# PART 3 — AGENT CONTRACT ARCHITECTURE

## 3.1 Contract Definition

The Agent Contract is the formal agreement between GeFlow's application/backend and a specialized agent.

It defines:

- Request structure.
- Identity verification.
- Tenant context.
- Category context.
- Conversation context.
- Authorized capabilities.
- Tool access.
- Execution constraints.
- Response structure.
- Error structure.
- Execution status.
- Version compatibility.

An agent must not independently decide which tenant, business or account it is authorized to access.

GeFlow's trusted backend must establish and validate that context.

## 3.2 Contract Layers

Define the following logical contract layers.

| Layer | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Identity Contract | Agent identity and registry reference |
| Category Contract | Business category and mapping |
| Tenant Contract | Business and tenant isolation |
| Request Contract | Incoming request structure |
| Context Contract | Authorized business and conversation context |
| Capability Contract | Available agent capabilities |
| Tool Contract | Tool invocation and permission rules |
| Execution Contract | Runtime lifecycle and execution limits |
| Response Contract | Standardized agent response |
| Error Contract | Structured error handling |
| Event Contract | Execution status and event reporting |
| Memory Contract | Authorized memory operations |
| Audit Contract | Traceability and accountability |
| Version Contract | Compatibility and change management |

These are logical contract boundaries. Whether they are implemented as separate APIs, internal modules or shared types must be decided after reviewing the actual architecture.

