# PART 12 — RUNTIME EXECUTION LIFECYCLE

Define the complete request lifecycle.

## Stage 1 — Request Received

A user submits a request through an existing GeFlow interface or another explicitly authorized integration.

## Stage 2 — Request Validation

The request gateway validates its structure and required fields.

## Stage 3 — Authentication

The runtime verifies the authenticated user.

## Stage 4 — Tenant Authorization

The runtime verifies that the user is authorized to access the requested business.

## Stage 5 — Category Resolution

The category resolver retrieves the business's verified category.

## Stage 6 — Primary Agent Resolution

The system retrieves the designated primary agent from the approved mapping registry.

## Stage 7 — Agent Validation

The runtime verifies agent identity, lifecycle status, configuration and contract compatibility.

## Stage 8 — Context Preparation

The context manager retrieves the minimum required authorized context.

## Stage 9 — Capability Validation

The runtime establishes which capabilities are available for the request.

## Stage 10 — Agent Execution

The selected agent processes the request within its authorized boundaries.

## Stage 11 — Tool Request

If the agent needs business data or an operation, it submits a structured tool request.

## Stage 12 — Tool Authorization

The tool gateway independently checks permissions, ownership, risk and approval requirements.

## Stage 13 — Tool Execution

Only authorized tools execute.

## Stage 14 — Tool Result

The runtime returns the structured tool result to the agent.

## Stage 15 — Response Generation

The agent generates its final response using verified results and authorized context.

## Stage 16 — Response Validation

The runtime validates the response envelope and applies required safety checks.

## Stage 17 — Audit and Completion

The runtime records the appropriate execution metadata and final status.

## Stage 18 — User Response

The validated response is returned to the requesting user.

### Lifecycle Diagram

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[User Request] --> B[Request Gateway]
    B --> C[Request Validation]
    C --> D[Authentication and Authorization]
    D --> E[Tenant Validation]
    E --> F[Category Resolver]
    F --> G[Primary Agent Resolver]
    G --> H[Agent and Contract Validation]
    H --> I[Context Preparation]
    I --> J[Capability Validation]
    J --> K[Agent Execution]
    K --> L{Tool Required?}
    L -- No --> M[Response Generation]
    L -- Yes --> N[Tool Gateway]
    N --> O[Permission and Policy Validation]
    O --> P{Authorized?}
    P -- No --> Q[Structured Error or Approval]
    P -- Yes --> R[Tool Execution]
    R --> S[Tool Result]
    S --> K
    Q --> M
    M --> T[Response Validation]
    T --> U[Audit and Completion]
    U --> V[Return Response]
```

