# PART 17 — EXECUTION RELIABILITY AND PERFORMANCE

Define runtime policies for:

### 17.1 Timeouts
- Request validation timeout.
- Context retrieval timeout.
- Model execution timeout.
- Tool execution timeout.
- Overall execution deadline.

Actual numeric values must be established after reviewing current infrastructure and expected workload. Do not invent production limits.

### 17.2 Concurrency
- Per-tenant execution limits.
- Per-agent execution limits.
- Per-user execution limits.
- Provider concurrency limits.
- Tool-specific concurrency restrictions.

### 17.3 Rate Limiting
- User-level limits.
- Tenant-level limits.
- Agent-level limits.
- Tool-level limits.
- Provider-level limits.

### 17.4 Idempotency

Write operations must support an appropriate idempotency strategy.

Repeated requests must not accidentally:
- Create duplicate purchases.
- Apply the same stock adjustment twice.
- Record duplicate transactions.
- Trigger duplicate financial operations.

### 17.5 Cancellation

Document:
- User cancellation.
- Runtime cancellation.
- Timeout cancellation.
- Tool cancellation where supported.
- Behavior when cancellation occurs after a write operation has already committed.

### 17.6 Circuit Breaking and Degradation

Define behavior when:
- AI providers are unavailable.
- Database services are unavailable.
- Tool services are unavailable.
- Context retrieval fails.
- A particular agent is suspended.

A failure in one agent must not automatically compromise unrelated agents.

